---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="How Deep Should a Gravel Driveway Be? (3-Layer Construction Guide) | CalcForDIY"
  description="Learn the exact layer-by-layer depths for a gravel driveway: 4-6 inches of #3 ballast sub-base, 3 inches of #57 crushed stone, and 2 inches of crusher run / DGA top coat."
>

  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline>
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    "itemListElement": [
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://calcfordiy.com" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Guides", "item": "https://calcfordiy.com/guides" },
      { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "How Deep Should a Gravel Driveway Be? (3-Layer Construction Guide)", "item": "https://calcfordiy.com/guides/how-deep-should-a-gravel-driveway-be" }
    ]
  }
  </script>
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline>
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": "How Deep Should a Gravel Driveway Be?",
    "description": "Comprehensive engineering guide to gravel driveway depth, aggregate layers (#3 ballast, #57 stone, crusher run), and geotextile fabric.",
    "author": {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "name": "CalcForDIY Earthwork Division"
    },
    "publisher": {
      "@type": "Organization",
      "name": "CalcForDIY",
      "logo": {
        "@type": "ImageObject",
        "url": "https://calcfordiy.com/favicon.svg"
      }
    },
    "mainEntityOfPage": "https://calcfordiy.com/guides/how-deep-should-a-gravel-driveway-be"
  }
  </script>

  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline>
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": [
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "How thick should a gravel driveway be?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "A new gravel driveway built from bare dirt must be 8 to 10 inches thick total, consisting of 3 distinct aggregate layers over heavy woven geotextile fabric. Resurfacing an existing stable driveway requires a 2 to 3-inch top coat of crusher run or #57 stone."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why do gravel driveways get potholes?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Potholes form when poor drainage allows standing water to soften the stone base, or when the driveway lacks a solid sub-base layer of large 2-3\" ballast rock, causing smaller gravel to push down into soft dirt under vehicle tire weight."
        }
      }
    ]
  }
  </script>

  <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-gray-500 mb-4 print:hidden">
    <a href="/" class="hover:text-blue-600">Home</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <a href="/guides" class="hover:text-blue-600">Guides</a>
    <span>/</span>
    <span>Gravel Driveway Depth</span>
  </div>

  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto">
    <header class="mb-8">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-full text-amber-800 text-xs font-semibold mb-3">
        <span>🚜 Earthwork &amp; Paving Standards</span>
        <span>•</span>
        <span>Trade-Verified Spec Guide</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-3 tracking-tight">
        How Deep Should a Gravel Driveway Be? (The 3-Layer Rule)
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        Building a gravel driveway that withstands heavy pickup trucks, delivery vans, and harsh winter freezes requires an 8-inch, 3-layer aggregate structure.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Quick Formula Box -->
    <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-amber-50 to-orange-50 border-2 border-amber-300 rounded-xl p-6 mb-8 shadow-xs">
      <h2 class="text-lg font-bold text-amber-950 mb-2 flex items-center gap-2">
        <span>🪨</span> The Gold Standard 3-Layer Driveway Structure (8" to 10" Total Depth):
      </h2>
      <ul class="text-sm text-amber-900 space-y-2 list-disc pl-5">
        <li><strong>Base Stabilization:</strong> Heavy-duty woven geotextile underlayment fabric directly over compacted subsoil.</li>
        <li><strong>Layer 1 — Sub-Base (4" to 6" Depth):</strong> Large <strong>#3 or #4 Ballast Stone (2" to 3" crushed rock)</strong> to create a solid bridge.</li>
        <li><strong>Layer 2 — Intermediate Layer (3" Depth):</strong> <strong>#57 Crushed Stone (3/4" angular stone)</strong> to fill voids and provide drainage.</li>
        <li><strong>Layer 3 — Top Surface Coat (2" Depth):</strong> <strong>Crusher Run / DGA (Dense Grade Aggregate)</strong> with stone dust to compact rock solid.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="mt-4 pt-3 border-t border-amber-200 flex justify-between items-center">
        <span class="text-xs text-amber-700 font-medium">Need to calculate total tons and dump truck deliveries?</span>
        <a href="/calculators/gravel-driveway" class="inline-flex items-center text-xs font-bold text-amber-800 hover:text-amber-900 bg-white px-3 py-1.5 rounded-lg border border-amber-300 shadow-2xs">
          Open Gravel Driveway Calculator →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- The 3 Critical Rules -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4">Why a Single Layer of Gravel Always Fails</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-4 leading-relaxed">
      A common DIY mistake is dumping 3 inches of 3/4" gravel directly onto raw dirt. Within a year, rainfall turns the dirt to mud, and vehicle tires push the loose stones down into the earth, resulting in deep ruts and potholes.
    </p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-8">
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 shadow-2xs">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-red-600 mb-2">❌ The Cheap Mistake (No Fabric &amp; 1 Layer)</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Poured directly on dirt. Vehicle tires displace stones into the soft soil. Potholes form everywhere within 6 months.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-5 shadow-2xs">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-green-700 mb-2">✅ The Engineered 3-Layer System</h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">Woven geotextile keeps rock separated from clay. Large ballast stone distributes 10,000 lb axle loads evenly. Lasts 15+ years.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Related Tools Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6 mb-8">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Related Driveway &amp; Paving Calculators</h3>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-3">
        <a href="/calculators/gravel-driveway" class="bg-white p-3.5 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 hover:border-blue-400 hover:shadow-xs transition-all">
          <div class="font-bold text-sm text-gray-900">Gravel Driveway Calculator</div>
          <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">Calculate tons, cubic yards &amp; tri-axle truckloads.</div>
        </a>
        <a href="/calculators/paver-walkway" class="bg-white p-3.5 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 hover:border-blue-400 hover:shadow-xs transition-all">
          <div class="font-bold text-sm text-gray-900">Paver Walkway Calculator</div>
          <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">Calculate connecting paths, sand &amp; edge restraints.</div>
        </a>
        <a href="/calculators/french-drain" class="bg-white p-3.5 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 hover:border-blue-400 hover:shadow-xs transition-all">
          <div class="font-bold text-sm text-gray-900">French Drain Calculator</div>
          <div class="text-xs text-gray-500 mt-1">Calculate driveway ditch stone &amp; pipe drainage.</div>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
